d{...}.\n\nA star-polygon is drawn on a clock face by drawing a chord from each number to the fifth number counted clockwise from that number.  That is, chords are drawn from 12 to 5, from 5 to 10, from 10 to 3, and so on, ending back at 12.  What is the degree measure of the angle at each vertex in the star-polygon?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"a18b65768b3723048ce3e1b134203cc188f9702caca5f97f9f6da390cbd952bc"},"seconds":33.147613763809204,"seed":162539356466624101,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve this problem, we need to understand the structure of the star-polygon and how it is formed on the clock face.\n\n1. **Identify the vertices and chords**: The star-polygon is formed by drawing chords from each number to the fifth number counted clockwise from that number. This means that each vertex of the star-polygon is one of the numbers on the clock face, and the chords connect these vertices in a specific pattern.\n\n2. **Determine the pattern**: The pattern of connections is as follows:\n   - From 12 to 5\n   - From 5 to 10\n   - From 10 to 3\n   - From 3 to 8\n   - From 8 to 1\n   - From 1 to 6\n   - From 6 to 11\n   - From 11 to 4\n   - From 4 to 9\n   - From 9 to 2\n   - From 2 to 7\n   - From 7 to 12\n\n   This pattern forms a star-polygon with 12 vertices.\n\n3. **Calculate the central angle**: The clock face is a circle, so the total angle around the center is 360 degrees. Each number on the clock face represents an angle of 30 degrees (360 degrees / 12 numbers = 30 degrees per number).\n\n4. **Determine the angle at each vertex**: The star-polygon is a 12-pointed star, and we need to find the internal angle at each vertex. The internal angle of a star-polygon with \\( n \\) points and a step size \\( k \\) (in this case, \\( n = 12 \\) and \\( k = 5 \\)) can be calculated using the formula:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Internal angle} = \\frac{180(n-2k)}{n}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\( n = 12 \\) and \\( k = 5 \\):\n   \\[\n   \\text{Internal angle} = \\frac{180(12-2 \\cdot 5)}{12} = \\frac{180(12-10)}{12} = \\frac{180 \\cdot 2}{12} = \\frac{360}{12} = 30\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the degree measure of the angle at each vertex in the star-polygon is \\(\\boxed{30}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{30}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
